{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700027,"nonce":"2QAVqRsSSYkOYf3BFPSI6rNXVw4=","title":"A Dirichlet pin on a point-set tag that reaches interior nodes is silently ignored","message":"Pinning a region defined by `domain.tag(name, predicate)` only constrains the nodes on the boundary; the interior nodes the predicate also selects keep their solved values, with no error or warning.\n\n**What goes wrong.** The `tag` docstring says a tag can name \"any subset of the domain, interior or boundary\", and a Dirichlet `u(*d.variable(name, split=True)[:2]) - g` reads as a pin of every node in it. But FEM location functions are evaluated against the boundary only, so only boundary nodes are constrained.\n\n**Reproduce.** Unit square, P1 Poisson `-lap u = 1`, `u = 0` on the boundary:\n```\nd = jno.shape.rect(0, 0, 1, 1, size=0.05).domain()\nd.tag(\"band\", lambda x, y: abs(x - 0.5) \u003c 0.06)      # crosses the square top to bottom\nx, y, _ = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True); b = d.variable(\"boundary\", split=True)\nk = d.variable(\"band\", split=True); u, v = d.fem_symbols()\na, t = u.bind(x=x, y=y), v.bind(x=x, y=y)\nfem = jno.fem([a.x*t.x + a.y*t.y - 1.0*t, u(b[0], b[1]) - 0.0, u(k[0], k[1]) - 2.0])\nsol = fem.solve()\n```\nAmong the 55 band nodes strictly inside the square, u ranges from 0.32 to 1.32; none is 2.0. A band wholly inside the square (`abs(x-.5)\u003c.06 \u0026 abs(y-.5)\u003c.06`) does not solve silently: it raises \"a residual with the trial but no test function must live on a boundary region ... Got the whole-domain volume\", which does not name the cause either. I ran the single-region case only; #250 covers the multi-region refusal,; a silent pin on a multi-region domain was reported but I did not reproduce it here.\n\n**Expected.** Either every node the predicate selects is pinned (the docstring's reading), or the library refuses the pin with a message that says interior points of a tag cannot carry a Dirichlet value and that only its boundary part would. A constraint that is quietly applied to a fraction of its nodes returns a wrong field that looks fine.\n\n**Where.** jno/domain/domain_class.py `tag` and `_register_tag_boundary_region` (what a tag resolves to on the mesh); the Dirichlet resolution in the FEM (boundary-only node lookup).\n\n**Workaround.** Pin interior nodes with a penalty/Lagrange term over a named interior sub-region, or tag a boundary subset only.\n\n**Direction.** Cheapest: at pin build, compare the tag's mesh nodes with the boundary nodes it resolved to and raise when they differ. Better: support interior-node Dirichlet pins by eliminating those DOFs.","files":null}]}